WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.880
Model-serving performance depends on several interacting factors: model size, quantization, context length, available memory, CPU, GPU, disk speed, thermal behavior, and concurrent use.

2
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:23.547
A model that feels responsive for one user on a workstation may not be suitable for a shared server, a heavier prompt workflow, or multiple simultaneous clients.

3
00:00:23.547 --> 00:00:29.552
Operators should watch memory, disk, CPU, GPU, and temperature while evaluating a model.

4
00:00:29.552 --> 00:00:34.925
Memory pressure can cause slow responses, failed loads, or system instability.

5
00:00:34.925 --> 00:00:38.244
Disk pressure can affect model pulls and logging.

6
00:00:38.244 --> 00:00:41.878
CPU and GPU pressure can change latency expectations.

7
00:00:41.878 --> 00:00:48.358
Thermal throttling can make performance worse over time even if the first few requests look fine.

8
00:00:48.358 --> 00:00:56.022
Context length also matters. Longer prompts and larger conversation histories require more work and more memory.

9
00:00:56.022 --> 00:01:06.610
Keep-alive behavior and loaded models can improve responsiveness because a model does not need to load for every request, but they can also hold scarce memory.

10
00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:15.302
The best setting depends on whether the environment is optimized for interactive use, occasional requests, or shared workloads.

11
00:01:15.302 --> 00:01:21.386
Capacity planning should be based on observed workloads rather than wishful assumptions.

12
00:01:21.386 --> 00:01:28.972
Measure representative prompts, expected concurrency, response latency, resource use, and failure behavior.

13
00:01:28.972 --> 00:01:32.211
Then document what the environment can support.

14
00:01:32.211 --> 00:01:39.955
Performance planning turns local AI from a pleasant demo into an operational service with realistic expectations.
